import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var n={particleInBox:{theory:{concept:"[[A particle in a box is the quantum model of a particle trapped between two impenetrable walls: only waves that fit exactly between the walls are allowed, so the particle can have only certain energies \u2014 its energy levels.]] A ball between two walls can move at any speed. An electron confined to a region a nanometre wide behaves as a wave, and only a wave with a whole number of half-wavelengths between the walls is possible. Each such wave is a level n with the energy E\u2099 = n\xB2h\xB2/(8mL\xB2): the narrower the box and the higher the level, the larger the energy. This simplest quantum model explains why atoms, molecules and nanocrystals have only certain energies and colours."},section:{trap:{title:"Trapped between two walls",text:"In the model an electron is in a straight box of width L. Inside, no force acts on it (V = 0); the walls are infinitely high (V = \u221E), so the electron can never be found outside the box. Physicists also call this an infinitely deep potential well. Real cases come close to it: an electron in a long dye molecule, in a thin semiconductor layer or in a nanocrystal a few nanometres across (a quantum dot). [[Because the electron cannot be outside the box, its wave must be zero at both walls.]]"},standing:{title:"Only waves that fit",text:"Every particle is also a wave with the de Broglie wavelength \u03BB = h/p. Like a guitar string fixed at both ends, the wave in the box must have nodes at the walls. [[Only a whole number of half-wavelengths fits into the box: L = n\xB7\u03BB/2.]] Level n = 1 is one hump (the ground state), n = 2 is two humps with a node in the middle, and in general level n has n \u2212 1 nodes inside the box. The line in the simulation shows this standing wave."},energy:{title:"Only certain energies",text:"A shorter wave means a larger momentum p = h/\u03BB and a larger kinetic energy E = p\xB2/2m. Putting in \u03BB = 2L/n gives [[E\u2099 = n\xB2\xB7h\xB2/(8mL\xB2): the energy grows as n\xB2 and as 1/L\xB2.]] Levels 2, 3 and 4 have 4, 9 and 16 times the energy of level 1, and a box half as wide makes every energy four times larger. Energies between the levels are impossible: the energy is quantised. Even the lowest level is above zero, so a trapped electron can never be completely at rest."},cloud:{title:"Where is the electron?",text:"The wave does not say exactly where the electron is. [[The chance of finding the electron at a place is given by |\u03C8|\xB2, the probability density]] \u2014 the filled curve. Each dot in the strip under the box is one measurement: an electron in an identical box is found at one place, at random. One dot tells little, but hundreds of dots pile up where |\u03C8|\xB2 is large. At a node |\u03C8|\xB2 = 0: on level 2 the electron is never found in the very middle of the box, although it is found on both sides of it. A classical ball bouncing between the walls would be found everywhere equally often."},still:{title:"A state that does not change",text:"On one level the wave function does change in time: the line, its real part, swings up and down like a vibrating guitar string, faster on higher levels (period h/E\u2099). But the change has the same rhythm at every point of the box, so |\u03C8|\xB2 stays exactly the same. [[A state with one definite energy is stationary: its probability cloud never moves.]] This is why an electron on an energy level of an atom does not lose energy and fall into the nucleus."},mix:{title:"Two levels at once",text:"An electron can also be in a superposition: a mixture of two levels, for example 1 and 2. The two waves interfere \u2014 in some places they add, in others they cancel \u2014 and because they oscillate at different rates, the pattern moves. [[In a superposition the probability cloud moves and repeats every T = h/(E_b \u2212 E_a).]] With levels 1 and 2 the electron sloshes from wall to wall and \u27E8x\u27E9 swings. With levels 1 and 3 (both symmetric about the centre) the cloud only breathes in and out, and \u27E8x\u27E9 stays in the middle. Measuring the energy of such an electron gives E_a or E_b, never anything in between, with probabilities equal to the shares."},light:{title:"Light from the box",text:"When the electron drops from level b to a lower level a, it gives out one photon that carries the energy difference: [[\u03BB_photon = hc/(E_b \u2212 E_a).]] The sloshing frequency (E_b \u2212 E_a)/h is exactly the frequency of this light: a charge that swings back and forth radiates. So only pairs whose cloud sloshes \u2014 one odd and one even level \u2014 give out light this way; a breathing pair such as 1 and 3 does not. A narrower box pushes the levels apart and gives bluer light. The same idea explains the spectral lines of atoms (see the topic \u201CAtomic energy levels and spectra\u201D)."},today:{title:"Boxes in modern technology",text:"Quantum dots are semiconductor crystals only a few nanometres across. The smaller the dot, the further apart its levels and the bluer the light it gives out, so the same material glows red, green or blue depending only on its size. [[In a tiny box, size decides colour.]] Quantum dots are used in QLED television screens and to mark cells in medical research; the 2023 Nobel Prize in Chemistry (Moungi Bawendi, Louis Brus and Alexei Ekimov) was awarded for discovering and making them. Electrons in long dye molecules also behave roughly like particles in a box, which is why the colour of a dye depends on the length of its molecule."},check:{title:"Check what you learned",text:"Can a trapped electron have zero energy? (No \u2014 even level 1 has E\u2081 > 0.) What happens to the energies if the box becomes half as wide? (They become four times larger.) How many times more energy does level 3 have than level 1? (Nine.) Where is the electron on level 2 never found? (In the very middle and at the walls.) Does the probability cloud of one level move? (No \u2014 it is stationary; only a superposition moves.) Check each answer in the simulation."}},formula:{fit:"Only a whole number of half-waves fits into the box",energy:"Energy of level n: grows as n\xB2, falls as 1/L\xB2",wave:"Wave function of level n (zero at both walls)",probability:"Probability of finding the electron between two points",period:"Period of the motion in a superposition of levels a and b",photon:"Wavelength of the photon emitted in a jump from b to a",schrodinger:"Schr\xF6dinger equation inside the box, solved by every \u03C8\u2099"},variable:{planck:"Planck constant h = 6.626\xB710\u207B\xB3\u2074 J\xB7s",mass:"Mass of the electron, 9.11\xB710\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg",psi:"Wave function; the line shows its real part",density:"Probability density: where the electron is likely to be found"},assumption:{oneDimension:"The electron moves along one line. It is free and non-relativistic, and no force acts on it inside the box.",walls:"The walls are infinitely high and perfectly hard. Real walls are finite, so the wave leaks a little into them (see \u201CQuantum tunneling\u201D) and the levels lie slightly lower.",mix:"The shares of a superposition are set directly, with real weights; how such a state is prepared is not modelled. Only the photon's wavelength is computed, not how long the electron takes to emit it.",detections:"Each dot is a different electron in an identical box, measured once: a measurement changes the state, so one electron cannot be measured over and over. The random numbers come from a fixed seed, so a run repeats exactly. In a superposition only the last few dots are shown, because the cloud moves.",slow:"The motion is slowed down enormously, and by a different amount for each box width: one swing of the ground state's wave always takes 8 s of animation, while in a 1 nm box it really takes about 11 femtoseconds (h/E\u2081). The readings give the real times.",drawing:"The curves are scaled to the same height for every box, so their height is not to scale, and the line shows only the real part of the complex wave function. The width of the box is drawn to scale, the energy levels on a fixed scale up to 10 eV."},param:{width:"Box width",level:"Energy level n",superposition:"Mix in a second level",secondLevel:"Second level",secondShare:"Share of the second level",timeStep:"Time step"},hint:{width:"Distance between the walls. A small molecule is about 0.5\u20131 nm long; a quantum dot is a few nanometres across.",level:"Which allowed wave the electron is in: n = 1 is the ground state, with the lowest energy.",superposition:"Put the electron on two levels at once (a superposition) and watch the probability cloud move.",secondLevel:"The other level of the superposition. If it is the same as the first one, nothing moves.",secondShare:"Probability that an energy measurement finds the second level. 50 % is an equal mixture."},preset:{narrow:"0.5 nm",medium:"1 nm",wide:"2 nm",ground:"n = 1 (ground state)",second:"n = 2",third:"n = 3"},measure:{energy:"Energy of level n",nodes:"Nodes inside the box",middle:"Probability in the middle tenth",detections:"Electrons measured",groundEnergy:"Ground-state energy",wavelength:"de Broglie wavelength of level n",secondEnergy:"Energy of the second level",left:"Probability in the left half",maxLeft:"Largest share in the left half",mean:"Expected position",phasePeriod:"Period of the wave (real time)",sloshPeriod:"Period of the motion (real time)",meanEnergy:"Average energy",physicalTime:"Real time",transitionEnergy:"Energy difference of the levels",photon:"Wavelength of the emitted photon"},graph:{position:"Where the electron is expected, over time",left:"Probability in the left half"},view:{ladder:"Energy levels",aboveScale:"Above the scale: {levels}",detector:"Measurements",detectorRecent:"Latest measurements",legend:"Filled: probability density |\u03C8|\xB2 \xB7 Line: the wave Re \u03C8 \xB7 Dots: measured electrons",legendMixed:"Filled: probability density |\u03C8|\xB2 \xB7 Line: the wave Re \u03C8 \xB7 Dots: the latest measured electrons",stationary:"Level n = {n}, E = {energy}: a stationary state \u2014 |\u03C8|\xB2 stays still, only the wave oscillates",slosh:"Levels {a} and {b} together: the electron sloshes from wall to wall (period {period})",breathe:"Levels {a} and {b} together: the cloud breathes symmetrically and \u27E8x\u27E9 stays in the middle (period {period})",finished:"{count} electrons measured, each in its own box: they gather where |\u03C8|\xB2 is large",realPartOption:"The wave (Re \u03C8)",detectionsOption:"Measured electrons",levelsOption:"Energy levels",meanOption:"Expected position \u27E8x\u27E9"},challenge:{squeeze:{title:"Squeeze the electron",description:"Keep the electron in the ground state (n = 1) and give it an energy of at least 1 eV.",hint:"There is no force to push it with here. Make the box narrower: the energy grows as 1/L\xB2."},middle:{title:"A hole in the middle",description:"Choose a single level on which the electron is found in the middle tenth of the box with a probability below 1 %.",hint:"Look for a level whose |\u03C8|\xB2 curve touches zero exactly at the centre."},same:{title:"Same energy, higher level",description:"Give the electron an energy of 3 eV (\xB1 0.1 eV) on level 2 or higher.",hint:"On level 1 this would take a box only 0.35 nm wide. On level n the box has to be n times wider."},humps:{title:"Three humps",description:"Measure all 400 electrons in a state whose |\u03C8|\xB2 has three humps, and look at where the dots pile up.",hint:"Three humps have two nodes between them. Choose that level and let the run finish."},slosh:{title:"From wall to wall",description:"Make a superposition in which, at some moments, the electron is in the left half with a probability of at least 90 %.",hint:"The two levels must differ in symmetry (one odd, one even) and lie close together. Try the lowest two."},breathe:{title:"Breathing, not sloshing",description:"Make a superposition whose cloud moves, but whose left half always holds exactly 50 %.",hint:"Mix two odd levels (or two even ones): each of them is symmetric about the centre."},period:{title:"Ten femtoseconds",description:"Make the electron slosh from wall to wall with a real period of 10 fs (\xB1 0.5 fs).",hint:"For levels 1 and 2, T = h/(3E\u2081) grows as L\xB2. Try a box about 1.65 nm wide."},light:{title:"Visible light",description:"Make a sloshing superposition whose jump down would emit visible light (400\u2013700 nm).",hint:"For levels 1 and 2 the photon is about 1100 nm in a 1 nm box and grows as L\xB2. Try a box about 0.7 nm wide."}}}},o={sim:n};export{o as default,n as sim};
